{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VSb8RkM3xnmJR4DYLXDQc4","type":"tool_result","content":"77:**7.** Most people's answer includes some subset of: a name, a version, a place to get it from, the ability to install without reading the contents, the ability to uninstall cleanly, and the ability to find out what is currently installed. Hold onto whichever ones you named. You will need them shortly.\n111:[Omitted long matching line]\n115:> **Logbook Entry:** Every operations team eventually accumulates the directory. You know the one. It is called `k8s/` or `deploy/` or, in the most honest cases, `yaml/`. Inside it there is `deployment.yaml`, and beside that `deployment-staging.yaml`, and beside *that* `deployment-prod-final-v2-USE-THIS-ONE.yaml`, whose name is a small monument to the moment somebody realized the naming scheme had stopped working and did not have time to fix it.\n219:Note what that definition contains and what it does not. It says *collection of files*, *versioned*, *deployable*. It does not say *templated*. Templating is in there, and we will get to it, but the definition of a chart does not require it, and that is not an accident of phrasing. Hold onto it; §7 is built on it.\n294:[Omitted long matching line]\n306:A special directory you can create in your chart to hold your CRDs [source: helm-crd-best-practices-2026-08-31]. These CRDs are **not templated**, and are installed by default when you run `helm install` for the chart [source: helm-crd-best-practices-2026-08-31].\n410:[Omitted long matching line]\n432:> Chart, release, revision. You have had this mental model since the first time you administered anything. Helm did not invent it; Helm brought it to Kubernetes, where the native tooling had objects but no packages. The reason the vocabulary is worth learning precisely is not that the concepts are hard, since you already hold them, but that Kubernetes had no word for any of it until Helm supplied three.\n452:> 🪝 **Snag:** Second half of the trap from §2, and this is the sentence to hold: `charts/` is a **directory inside a chart** containing the charts it depends on. A **chart repository** is an **HTTP server on the network** housing packaged charts. Same word, opposite ends of the pipeline: one is a component of a package, the other is a place packages are kept. If a question puts both in play, that is the distinction it is testing.\n454:### Registries hold charts too\n542:✓ Where charts live, and why registries turned out to hold more than images\n568:That last clause is the claim, and it is the one to hold onto: **without forking the originals**. The base is not edited. The base is not copied. Each overlay declares only its own differences and points at the base it differs from.\n700:So Helm carved out a directory. **`crds/` is a special directory you can create in your chart to hold your CRDs; these CRDs are not templated, but will be installed by default when running a `helm install` for the chart** [source: helm-crd-best-practices-2026-08-31]. Not templated, so there is nothing to render and nothing to sequence within. Installed by default, so the ordering is the tool's responsibility, not the README's.\n967:B) The OCI Distribution Specification defines an API for distributing *content*, not specifically images, so registries can hold other artifacts\n1032:[Omitted long matching line]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"735b6ad1-2e1d-4c5f-8159-8c93f33f19be","uuid":"7f0107bf-cbde-459b-a57c-245e395660a1","timestamp":"2026-08-31T08:57:56.168Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"77:**7.** Most people's answer includes some subset of: a name, a version, a place to get it from, the ability to install without reading the contents, the ability to uninstall cleanly, and the ability to find out what is currently installed. Hold onto whichever ones you named. You will need them shortly.\n111:[Omitted long matching line]\n115: